<h2>The award and the standoff</h2>
<p>An award of 31 July 2025 settled a conversion-arrangement dispute both ways: the respondent was declared entitled to the return of its raw materials, finished products and goods lying at the appellant\'s plant, and awarded ₹2.17 crore for a transformer; the appellant was awarded ₹18.87 crore in conversion charges plus a smaller post-termination sum, with 9% interest and a right of adjustment. The goods, however, stayed where they were. The award-holder\'s requests to inspect them in July and August 2026 went nowhere, and it moved the Delhi High Court under Section 9 for inventorisation. The Single Judge appointed two Local Commissioners on 14 October 2026; the owner of the premises appealed under Section 37(1)(b).</p>

<h2>The three objections, and how they fell</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>"Execution is pending at Visakhapatnam."</strong> Pendency is not enforcement. Section 9 operates until the award is enforced; the proceedings there — instituted after the OMP, the court noted — did not exhaust the Delhi court\'s jurisdiction.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>"A bailiff already made an inventory."</strong> Both bailiff and Local Commissioners are officers of the court; there was no legal bar to a second, fuller exercise, and no reason for apprehension absent any intent to tamper.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>"Section 47 CPC ousts the Single Judge."</strong> Section 47 speaks of questions "determined" by the executing court, and determination connotes finality. An inventorisation order expresses no opinion and decides nothing.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>"The order is unreasoned."</strong> The Single Judge had explained that inspection had been denied despite an award recognising entitlement — reasoning to which, the Bench said, no possible legal cavil could be taken.</div>
</div>

<h2>The doctrinal core</h2>
<p>Two propositions carry the judgment. First, the word "information" in Section 9(1)(ii)(c) is compendious: the particulars of goods — their identity, condition, quantity and commingling — are information the court may order to be ascertained in aid of preservation. Second, no restraint on Section 9 powers during execution can be introduced by judicial fiat where the statute contains none. Together they make the post-award window genuinely useful: an award-holder facing stonewalling need not wait for the executing court\'s timetable to secure the subject matter against dissipation or deterioration.</p>

<h2>Using the post-award window well</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Document the denial:</strong> the inspection requests of July and August 2026, met with silence, were the factual foundation of the relief. Make the record before moving the court.</li>
  <li><strong>Ask for information-type measures:</strong> inventories, inspections, preservation of records and samples sit squarely within Section 9(1)(ii)(c) and are harder to resist than injunctions.</li>
  <li><strong>Coordinate with execution:</strong> relief that preserves and records does not collide with the executing court; relief that distributes or adjudicates might. Frame accordingly.</li>
  <li><strong>Think twice before appealing:</strong> an in limine dismissal with a Division Bench\'s reasoned endorsement strengthens the order you challenged. Section 37 appeals against discretionary preservation orders need a jurisdictional error, not displeasure.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Award-holders whose awards concern physical goods should treat inventorisation as the first enforcement step, not an afterthought — commingling and deterioration arguments grow stronger for the resisting party with every month the goods sit unverified.</p>
</div>
